package com.nulljosh.nimble

/**
 * BC Law Studies 12 with no network: foundations of law, the Charter, criminal law and procedure, youth justice,
 * torts, contracts, family law and Indigenous rights. A port of `law12()` in docs/engine.js; the tables are copied from there by
 * script.
 */
internal object LawStudies12 {
    /** Phrase questions, matched anywhere. */
    private val facts = listOf(
        """\bequality rights\b""" to "Section 15 of the Charter: every individual is equal before and under the law and has the right to equal protection and benefit of the law without discrimination, in particular based on race, national or ethnic origin, colour, religion, sex, age or mental or physical disability. Courts have added grounds such as sexual orientation. Section 15(2) allows affirmative action programs.",
        """\bmobility rights\b""" to "Section 6 of the Charter: every citizen has the right to enter, remain in and leave Canada, and every citizen and permanent resident can move to and take up residence and work in any province.",
        """\bdemocratic rights\b""" to "Sections 3 to 5 of the Charter: every citizen has the right to vote and run in federal and provincial elections, no government can sit more than five years without an election except in war or emergency, and Parliament and legislatures must sit at least once a year.",
        """\bage of (?:sexual )?consent\b""" to "16 in Canada, under the Criminal Code. There are close-in-age exceptions (a 14 or 15 year old with a partner less than five years older; a 12 or 13 year old with a partner less than two years older), and it is 18 where there is a relationship of trust, authority or dependency.",
        """\bdisclosure\b.*\b(?:criminal|crown|trial|case|accused)\b""" to "The Crown's duty to give the defence all relevant evidence it has, whether it helps or hurts the accused, so the accused can make full answer and defence. The Supreme Court set this out in R v Stinchcombe (1991).",
        """\bbattery\b.*\b(?:tort|law|legal|civil)\b""" to "An intentional tort of harmful or offensive physical contact with another person without their consent, such as a punch or an unwanted touch. Assault, in tort, is the threat that makes someone expect it.",
        """\btreat(?:y|ies)\b.*\b(?:canadian law|in canada|first nations|indigenous|aboriginal|the crown)\b""" to "A formal agreement between the Crown and Indigenous nations setting out rights to land, resources and self-government. Historic treaties include the Numbered Treaties (1871 to 1921) and the Douglas Treaties on Vancouver Island; modern treaties include the Nisga'a Final Agreement (2000). Section 35 of the Constitution protects treaty rights.",
        """\bcommon[- ]law (?:relationship|partners?|spouses?|marriage|couples?)\b""" to "In BC, two people who have lived together in a marriage-like relationship for at least two years are spouses under the Family Law Act, with the same rights to divide property and claim spousal support as married couples (less than two years with a child counts for support).",
        """^a (?:last )?will(?: and testament)?$|^(?:a |the )?last will(?: and testament)?$""" to "A legal document in which a person (the will-maker or testator) says who gets their property and who manages their estate after they die. In BC it must be in writing, signed by the will-maker and two witnesses at the same time, under the Wills, Estates and Succession Act.",
        """\bfundamental freedoms\b""" to "Section 2 of the Charter: freedom of conscience and religion; freedom of thought, belief, opinion and expression, including freedom of the press; freedom of peaceful assembly; and freedom of association.",
        """\blegal rights\b.*\bcharter\b|\bcharter\b.*\blegal rights\b""" to "Sections 7 to 14 of the Charter: the right to life, liberty and security of the person (7), to be secure against unreasonable search or seizure (8), not to be arbitrarily detained (9), to be told the reason for arrest and to get a lawyer (10), to a fair and public trial within a reasonable time and the presumption of innocence (11), against cruel and unusual punishment (12), against self-incrimination (13) and to an interpreter (14).",
        """\bmurder\b.*\bmanslaughter\b|\bmanslaughter\b.*\bmurder\b""" to "Both are unlawful killings. Murder needs intent: the accused meant to kill, or meant to cause harm they knew was likely to kill. Manslaughter is an unlawful killing without that intent, such as from an unlawful act or criminal negligence, or murder reduced by provocation. Murder carries a mandatory life sentence; manslaughter has no minimum, except with a firearm.",
        """\bminors?\b.*\bcontracts?\b|\bcontracts?\b.*\bminors?\b""" to "In BC a minor is anyone under 19. Under the Infants Act most contracts with a minor cannot be enforced against them, though the minor can enforce them, and contracts for necessaries such as food, clothing and shelter must be paid at a reasonable price.",
        """\bsmall claims\b""" to "BC's Provincial Court Small Claims handles civil claims from \$5,001 to \$35,000, with simpler rules so people can act without a lawyer. Claims of \$5,000 or less go to the online Civil Resolution Tribunal.",
        """\b(?:minimum |youngest )?age\b.*\b(?:charged|criminally responsible|criminal responsibility|prosecuted)\b|\bage of criminal responsibility\b""" to "12. Under the Criminal Code no one under 12 can be convicted of an offence, and 12 to 17 year olds are dealt with under the Youth Criminal Justice Act.",
        """\bhow many (?:people|jurors|members|persons)\b.*\bjury\b|\bjury\b.*\bhow many (?:people|jurors|members)\b""" to "12 on a criminal jury in Canada, and the verdict must be unanimous. A civil jury in BC has 8 jurors.",
        """\bage of majority\b""" to "19 in British Columbia (and in Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, Newfoundland and Labrador and the territories); 18 in Alberta, Saskatchewan, Manitoba, Ontario, Quebec and PEI. At that age you can sign binding contracts and are an adult in law.",
        """\bpublic (?:and|vs\.?|versus) private law\b|\bprivate (?:and|vs\.?|versus) public law\b""" to "Public law governs the relationship between people and the government, and between parts of government: constitutional, criminal and administrative law. Private (civil) law governs relationships between individuals and organizations: contracts, torts, property and family law.",
        """\b(?:elements|requirements|parts|essentials) of (?:a |an )?(?:valid |legal |binding )?contract\b""" to "Offer, acceptance, consideration (something of value given by each side), capacity (both parties are legally able, such as adults of sound mind), legality of purpose, and intention to create legal relations.",
        """\bconsideration\b.*\bcontract\b|\bcontract\b.*\bconsideration\b""" to "Something of value each party gives or promises in exchange for the other's promise, such as money, goods, services or a promise to do or not do something. Without it, a promise is a gift, not a contract.",
        """\bsection 1 of (?:the )?charter\b|\bcharter section 1\b|\breasonable limits clause\b""" to "The reasonable limits clause: the Charter's rights and freedoms are guaranteed subject only to \"such reasonable limits prescribed by law as can be demonstrably justified in a free and democratic society.\" Courts test limits with the Oakes test.",
        """\bsection 35\b.*\bconstitution\b|\bconstitution\b.*\bsection 35\b""" to "Section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982 recognizes and affirms the existing Aboriginal and treaty rights of the Aboriginal peoples of Canada, the First Nations, Inuit and Métis.",
        """\b(?:defen[cs]e of )?self[- ]defen[cs]e\b.*\b(?:criminal|law|code)\b|\bdefen[cs]e of self[- ]defen[cs]e\b""" to "Section 34 of the Criminal Code: a person is not guilty if they reasonably believed force or a threat of force was being used against them or someone else, acted to defend or protect themselves, and what they did was reasonable in the circumstances.",
    ).map { Regex(it.first) to it.second }

    /** Legal terms, matched as the whole term by Terms. */
    private val terms = listOf(
        """(?:the )?rule of law""" to "The principle that everyone, including the government and its officials, is subject to the law and equal before it, and that laws must be public, clear and applied fairly by independent courts.",
        """common law""" to "Law made by judges through their decisions in court cases, which become precedents later courts follow (stare decisis). Canada outside Quebec uses common law, inherited from England.",
        """statute law|statutes?|statutory law""" to "Written law passed by Parliament or a provincial legislature, such as the Criminal Code or the BC Motor Vehicle Act. Where a statute and common law conflict, the statute wins.",
        """stare decisis""" to "Latin for \"to stand by things decided\": courts follow precedent, the earlier decisions of higher courts on the same legal question, so the law is predictable.",
        """actus reus""" to "The \"guilty act\": the physical part of a crime, the act or failure to act (omission) that the law forbids. With mens rea, it must be proven for most crimes.",
        """mens rea""" to "The \"guilty mind\": the mental part of a crime, such as intent, knowledge or recklessness. The Crown must prove it along with the actus reus for most crimes.",
        """(?:a )?summary(?: conviction)? offen[cs]es?""" to "A less serious crime, such as causing a disturbance, tried quickly in provincial court without a jury or preliminary inquiry. The usual maximum is two years less a day and a \$5,000 fine.",
        """(?:an )?indictable offen[cs]es?""" to "A serious crime, such as murder, robbery or sexual assault, with heavier penalties up to life in prison. The accused may get a preliminary inquiry and, for many offences, a choice of judge alone or judge and jury.",
        """(?:the )?presumption of innocence""" to "The rule, in section 11(d) of the Charter, that an accused person is innocent until proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt by the Crown.",
        """(?:proof )?beyond (?:a )?reasonable doubt""" to "The standard of proof in a criminal trial: the judge or jury must be sure the accused committed the offence, more than just probably guilty, though absolute certainty is not needed. The Crown carries this burden.",
        """(?:the )?burden of proof|onus of proof""" to "The duty to prove the case. In a criminal trial it is on the Crown, beyond a reasonable doubt; in a civil case it is on the plaintiff, on a balance of probabilities.",
        """(?:the )?(?:crown|crown prosecutor|crown counsel|prosecutor|prosecution)""" to "The lawyer who prosecutes criminal cases on behalf of the state (the Crown), presenting the evidence against the accused. In BC it is the BC Prosecution Service.",
        """bail|(?:a )?judicial interim release""" to "Release of an accused person from custody while they await trial, sometimes with conditions or a cash deposit. Under the Charter (section 11(e)) an accused has the right not to be denied reasonable bail without just cause.",
        """(?:a )?plea (?:bargain|bargaining|agreement|deal)""" to "A deal between the Crown and the defence in which the accused pleads guilty, usually in return for a lesser charge or a lighter recommended sentence, saving the cost of a trial.",
        """(?:the )?youth criminal justice act|(?:the )?ycja""" to "The 2003 federal law for young people aged 12 to 17 who break the law. It stresses rehabilitation and reintegration, uses police warnings and community programs instead of court where it can, protects the youth's identity, and keeps custody for violent and repeat offences.",
        """(?:the )?defen[cs]e of duress|duress""" to "A defence for someone who committed an offence because they were threatened with death or serious harm and had no safe way out. It does not cover murder and some other serious crimes.",
        """(?:the )?defen[cs]e of necessity""" to "A defence for breaking the law in an urgent emergency, to avoid imminent danger, when there was no reasonable legal alternative and the harm done was less than the harm avoided.",
        """(?:a )?jury|juries""" to "A group of citizens chosen at random who hear the evidence in a trial and decide the facts and the verdict. A criminal jury in Canada has 12 members and must be unanimous.",
        """(?:a )?torts?""" to "A civil wrong, other than a breached contract, that causes harm to a person or their property, for which the victim can sue for damages. Examples: negligence, trespass, defamation, assault and battery.",
        """negligence""" to "A tort in which someone fails to take the care a reasonable person would, causing foreseeable harm. The plaintiff must prove a duty of care, a breach of the standard of care, causation and actual damage.",
        """(?:a )?duty of care""" to "A legal obligation to take reasonable care not to cause foreseeable harm to others, such as a driver's duty to other road users. It is the first thing to prove in negligence, tested since Donoghue v Stevenson by the neighbour principle.",
        """defamation|libel|slander""" to "A tort of making a false statement that harms someone's reputation. Written or broadcast defamation is libel; spoken defamation is slander. Truth (justification) and fair comment are defences.",
        """(?:a )?contracts?""" to "A legally binding agreement between two or more parties that the courts will enforce, made up of an offer, acceptance and consideration between parties with capacity, for a legal purpose.",
        """(?:the )?balance of probabilities""" to "The standard of proof in civil cases: the plaintiff wins if their version is more likely than not true (over 50 percent), a lower bar than beyond a reasonable doubt.",
        """(?:a )?plaintiffs?|(?:a )?claimants?""" to "The person or organization who starts a civil lawsuit, suing the defendant for a remedy such as damages.",
        """(?:the )?(?:bc |british columbia )?human rights code""" to "BC's law against discrimination in employment, housing, services and membership, on grounds such as race, sex, religion, disability, sexual orientation, gender identity and age. Complaints go to the BC Human Rights Tribunal.",
        """aboriginal title|indigenous title""" to "The inherent right of Indigenous peoples to their ancestral lands, including the right to use and control them. The Supreme Court first declared title to specific land in Tsilhqot'in Nation v British Columbia in 2014.",
        """(?:an )?appeals?""" to "Asking a higher court to review a lower court's decision for an error of law or, sometimes, fact. In BC appeals go to the BC Court of Appeal and then, with permission, to the Supreme Court of Canada.",
        """(?:the )?supreme court of canada""" to "Canada's highest court and final court of appeal, in Ottawa, with nine judges, three of them from Quebec. It hears about 40 to 80 cases a year, chosen for national importance.",
        """(?:the )?notwithstanding clause|section 33(?: of the charter)?""" to "Section 33 of the Charter, which lets Parliament or a provincial legislature pass a law that overrides certain Charter rights (sections 2 and 7 to 15) for five years at a time, renewable.",
        """(?:the )?(?:canadian )?charter(?: of rights and freedoms)?""" to "The part of the Constitution Act, 1982 that guarantees rights and freedoms, including fundamental freedoms, democratic, mobility, legal and equality rights and language rights, subject to reasonable limits (section 1) and the notwithstanding clause (section 33).",
        """civil law""" to "The law of private disputes between people and organizations, such as contracts, torts, property and family law, where the remedy is usually money damages. It also names Quebec's code-based legal system, from French law.",
        """criminal law""" to "Public law that defines crimes against society and sets their punishments, mainly in the federal Criminal Code. The Crown prosecutes, and guilt must be proven beyond a reasonable doubt.",
        """(?:the )?criminal code(?: of canada)?""" to "The federal statute that defines most crimes in Canada, their procedures and their penalties, first passed in 1892. Criminal law is a federal power under the Constitution Act, 1867, so it applies everywhere in Canada.",
        """case law|judge[- ]made law""" to "Law made by the decisions of judges in past cases, which serve as precedents for later cases. Another name for common law.",
        """(?:a )?(?:legal )?precedents?""" to "An earlier court decision that is followed when a later case raises the same legal question. Lower courts must follow the precedents of higher courts in their system (stare decisis).",
        """(?:the )?constitution act,? 1867|(?:the )?british north america act|(?:the )?bna act""" to "The act of the British Parliament that created Canada at Confederation on July 1, 1867. It sets up Parliament and the provinces and divides their powers: section 91 lists federal powers, such as criminal law and defence, and section 92 provincial ones, such as education and health.",
        """habeas corpus""" to "Latin for \"you have the body\": the right of a detained person to go before a judge, who decides whether the detention is lawful and orders release if it is not. Section 10(c) of the Charter protects it.",
        """(?:the )?right to (?:counsel|a lawyer)""" to "Section 10(b) of the Charter: on arrest or detention, everyone has the right to retain and instruct a lawyer without delay and to be told of that right. Police must give a chance to call one, including free legal aid.",
        """(?:an )?unreasonable search and seizure|search and seizure|section 8(?: of the charter)?""" to "Section 8 of the Charter protects everyone against unreasonable search or seizure. A search is usually reasonable only with a warrant or under a clear legal power, and evidence found in an unreasonable search can be excluded under section 24(2).",
        """(?:a )?hybrid offen[cs]es?|(?:a )?dual procedure offen[cs]es?""" to "An offence the Crown can choose to prosecute either as a summary conviction offence or as an indictable offence, depending on how serious the case is, such as impaired driving or assault. Most offences in the Criminal Code are hybrid.",
        """first[- ]degree murder""" to "Murder that is planned and deliberate, or the murder of a police officer or prison guard, or committed during certain crimes like sexual assault or kidnapping. The sentence is life in prison with no parole for 25 years.",
        """second[- ]degree murder""" to "Intentional murder that is not planned and deliberate. The sentence is life in prison, with no parole for 10 to 25 years, as the judge sets.",
        """manslaughter""" to "An unlawful killing without the intent needed for murder, such as a death caused by an unlawful act or criminal negligence. The maximum is life, with no minimum unless a firearm was used.",
        """(?:a )?preliminary (?:inquiry|hearing)""" to "A hearing before a judge for some serious indictable offences to decide if the Crown has enough evidence to send the accused to trial. Since 2019 it is limited to offences with a maximum of life in prison.",
        """(?:a )?(?:search |arrest )?warrants?""" to "A document signed by a judge or justice of the peace that authorizes the police to arrest a named person or to search a named place for evidence.",
        """(?:an )?acquittals?|(?:an )?acquitted""" to "A finding by the judge or jury that the accused is not guilty, because the Crown did not prove the charge beyond a reasonable doubt. The accused is released and cannot be tried again for the same offence (double jeopardy), unless the Crown wins an appeal.",
        """(?:a )?conditional sentences?""" to "A prison sentence of under two years that the offender serves in the community under strict conditions, often house arrest, instead of in jail. Breaking the conditions can send them to jail.",
        """restorative justice""" to "An approach to crime that focuses on repairing the harm done: the offender, the victim and the community meet, often in a circle, and agree on how the offender will make amends. It draws on Indigenous justice traditions.",
        """(?:a )?youth sentences?""" to "A sentence under the Youth Criminal Justice Act for 12 to 17 year olds, focused on rehabilitation: reprimands, fines, community service, probation, intensive support, and custody only for violent or repeat offences, with shorter maximums than adults. A youth can get an adult sentence for the most serious crimes.",
        """(?:the )?defen[cs]e of intoxication|(?:the )?intoxication defen[cs]e""" to "Being drunk or high is a partial defence only for specific intent offences, such as murder, which can be reduced to manslaughter. Under section 33.1 of the Criminal Code it is not a defence to violent general intent offences unless the intoxication was extreme and not foreseeable.",
        """(?:the )?(?:insanity|mental disorder) defen[cs]e|not criminally responsible(?: on account of mental disorder)?|ncrmd""" to "Under section 16 of the Criminal Code, a person is not criminally responsible if a mental disorder made them unable to appreciate the nature of their act or to know it was wrong. They are not convicted, and a review board decides on hospital or release.",
        """(?:an )?alibi""" to "A defence that the accused was somewhere else when the crime took place, so could not have done it. It should be disclosed to the Crown early so it can be checked.",
        """entrapment""" to "A defence where police offered someone the chance to commit a crime without a reasonable suspicion they were already involved, or pushed them into it. If proven, the court stays the charges.",
        """(?:a )?hung jur(?:y|ies)""" to "A jury that cannot reach a unanimous verdict after long deliberation. The judge declares a mistrial, and the Crown can try the accused again.",
        """(?:an )?intentional torts?""" to "A tort done on purpose, such as assault (a threat of harm), battery (harmful contact), false imprisonment, trespass and defamation, as opposed to negligence.",
        """vicarious liability""" to "Responsibility for someone else's wrong, most often an employer for the torts its employees commit while doing their jobs, even if the employer did nothing wrong itself.",
        """contributory negligence""" to "When the plaintiff's own carelessness helped cause their injury, such as not wearing a seatbelt. In BC the damages are reduced by the plaintiff's share of fault under the Negligence Act.",
        """occupiers'? liability|occupier'?s liability""" to "The duty of whoever controls a property to keep it reasonably safe for people who come onto it. In BC the Occupiers Liability Act sets this duty, with a lower one for trespassers.",
        """(?:civil )?damages""" to "Money a court orders the defendant to pay to compensate the plaintiff for a loss or injury: general damages for pain and suffering, special damages for actual costs like lost wages, and rarely punitive damages to punish.",
        """(?:a )?breach of contract""" to "When a party fails to do what the contract requires, without lawful excuse. The other party can sue for damages, and for a serious breach may end the contract or seek an order to perform it.",
        """(?:a )?defendants?|(?:the )?accused""" to "The person or organization sued in a civil case, or charged with an offence in a criminal case (also called the accused).",
        """(?:the )?divorce act""" to "The federal law that governs divorce in Canada. The only ground is breakdown of the marriage, shown by living apart for a year, adultery or cruelty, and it covers parenting and support for divorcing couples.",
        """(?:the )?(?:bc )?family law act""" to "BC's law on family matters for married and unmarried couples: parenting arrangements based on the child's best interests, child and spousal support, and dividing family property and debt when a relationship ends.",
        """child support""" to "Money one parent pays the other to help with a child's living costs after they separate, usually set by the Federal Child Support Guidelines by the paying parent's income and the number of children.",
        """(?:the )?(?:bc )?court of appeal(?: for british columbia)?""" to "BC's highest court, which hears appeals from the BC Supreme Court and Provincial Court on errors of law. Appeals from it go to the Supreme Court of Canada with permission.",
        """(?:the )?canadian human rights act""" to "The 1977 federal law against discrimination by federally regulated employers and services, such as banks, airlines and the federal government. Complaints go to the Canadian Human Rights Commission.",
        """(?:the )?indian act""" to "The 1876 federal law that governs registered First Nations people (status Indians), reserves and band councils. It has been used to control and assimilate First Nations, including through residential schools, and is still in force with many amendments.",
        """administrative law""" to "The law governing how government agencies, boards and tribunals make decisions that affect people, such as the Labour Relations Board or the Residential Tenancy Branch, and how courts can review those decisions for fairness.",
        """constitutional law""" to "The law of the Constitution: how government is set up, the division of powers between Parliament and the provinces, and the rights in the Charter. Courts can strike down laws that conflict with it.",
        """(?:the )?division of powers""" to "The split of law-making power between Parliament and the provinces in the Constitution Act, 1867. Section 91 gives Ottawa matters like criminal law, defence, banking and citizenship; section 92 gives the provinces matters like education, health, property and civil rights, and municipalities.",
        """double jeopardy""" to "The rule, in section 11(h) of the Charter, that a person who has been finally acquitted or convicted of an offence cannot be tried or punished for it again.",
        """(?:an )?arraignment""" to "The start of a criminal trial, when the charge is read to the accused in court and they enter a plea of guilty or not guilty.",
        """(?:the )?right to (?:remain )?silen(?:t|ce)""" to "The right of an accused or detained person not to say anything to police, protected by section 7 of the Charter, and not to testify at their own trial, section 11(c). Silence cannot be used as evidence of guilt.",
        """(?:a )?voir dire""" to "A \"trial within a trial\": a hearing held without the jury to decide if a piece of evidence, such as a confession, is admissible.",
        """hearsay(?: evidence)?""" to "Evidence of a statement someone made outside court, offered to prove that what it says is true, such as a witness saying what another person told them. It is usually not allowed, with exceptions when it is necessary and reliable.",
        """(?:a )?mistrials?""" to "A trial that is ended before a verdict and declared invalid, because of a serious error, misconduct or a hung jury. The accused can usually be tried again.",
        """(?:an )?absolute discharge|(?:a )?conditional discharge""" to "A sentence where the accused is found guilty but not convicted, so they have no conviction. An absolute discharge has no conditions; a conditional one comes with probation conditions. The record is removed after one year (absolute) or three (conditional).",
        """(?:a )?peace bonds?|(?:a )?section 810(?: order)?""" to "A court order under section 810 of the Criminal Code requiring someone to keep the peace and be of good behaviour, often with conditions like no contact, for up to a year, when there are grounds to fear they will cause harm. It is not a conviction.",
        """criminal negligence""" to "Doing, or failing to do, something that shows wanton or reckless disregard for the lives or safety of others (section 219 of the Criminal Code), such as causing death by criminal negligence, punishable by up to life in prison.",
        """break(?:ing)? and enter(?:ing)?|b and e""" to "Breaking into a place such as a house or building to commit an offence there, or breaking out after committing one (section 348 of the Criminal Code). Breaking into a home is indictable with a maximum of life.",
        """impaired driving|drunk driving|dui""" to "Operating a vehicle while your ability is impaired by alcohol or drugs, or with a blood alcohol level of 80 mg per 100 mL or more, a Criminal Code offence. In BC, police can also issue an Immediate Roadside Prohibition at 50 mg.",
        """extrajudicial (?:measures|sanctions)""" to "Ways under the Youth Criminal Justice Act to deal with a young offender outside court: police warnings, cautions and referrals to community programs (measures), or a formal program the youth must complete (sanctions). They are presumed right for a first non-violent offence.",
        """strict liability(?: offen[cs]es?)?""" to "An offence, usually a regulatory one like a pollution or safety violation, where the Crown does not need to prove intent: the accused is liable unless they prove due diligence, that they took all reasonable care. Absolute liability offences allow no such defence.",
        """trespass(?: to land)?""" to "Entering or staying on someone's land without permission, a tort even with no damage done. Under BC's Trespass Act it can also be an offence after notice to stay off.",
        """(?:the )?standard of care""" to "The level of care a reasonable person would take in the situation. In negligence, the defendant breaches the duty of care by falling below it; professionals are held to the standard of a reasonable member of their profession.",
        """(?:a )?misrepresentations?""" to "A false statement of fact that leads someone to enter a contract. If it is innocent, the contract may be cancelled; if it is fraudulent or negligent, the victim can also sue for damages.",
        """(?:a )?void contracts?""" to "A contract that has no legal effect from the start and cannot be enforced by either party, such as one for an illegal purpose.",
        """(?:a )?voidable contracts?""" to "A valid contract that one party may choose to cancel (set aside), for example because of misrepresentation, duress, undue influence or because they were a minor.",
        """(?:a )?leases?|(?:a )?tenancy agreements?""" to "A contract in which a landlord lets a tenant use property, such as an apartment, for a set time in exchange for rent. In BC residential leases are governed by the Residential Tenancy Act.",
        """(?:the )?(?:bc )?residential tenancy act""" to "BC's law on renting homes: tenancy agreements, rent increases (capped yearly), security deposits (no more than half a month's rent), repairs, eviction notices and disputes, which go to the Residential Tenancy Branch.",
        """spousal support""" to "Money one spouse pays the other after separation to help with living costs, based on need, ability to pay and the roles each played in the relationship, often guided by the Spousal Support Advisory Guidelines.",
        """(?:the )?duty to consult(?: and accommodate)?""" to "The Crown's legal duty to consult Indigenous peoples, and accommodate their concerns where needed, before decisions that could affect their Aboriginal or treaty rights, set out in Haida Nation v British Columbia (2004).",
        """undrip|(?:the )?(?:united nations |un )?declaration on the rights of indigenous peoples""" to "The United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, adopted in 2007, setting out rights to self-determination, culture, lands and free, prior and informed consent. BC made it law with DRIPA in 2019, and Canada in 2021.",
        """theft|stealing""" to "Taking or using someone else's property without their consent, with the intent to deprive them of it (section 322 of the Criminal Code). Theft over \$5,000 is indictable with up to 10 years; theft under \$5,000 is a hybrid offence.",
        """robbery""" to "Theft with violence or threats of violence, or while armed with a weapon (section 343 of the Criminal Code). It is indictable, with a maximum of life in prison.",
        """assault""" to "Intentionally applying force to someone without their consent, or threatening to with the ability to carry it out (section 265 of the Criminal Code). More serious forms are assault with a weapon or causing bodily harm, and aggravated assault.",
        """sexual assault""" to "Any sexual contact without consent (sections 271 to 273 of the Criminal Code), from unwanted touching to rape. Consent must be voluntary, ongoing and can be withdrawn at any time; someone who is unconscious or intoxicated cannot consent.",
        """(?:the )?(?:oakes test)""" to "The test from R v Oakes (1986) for whether a law that limits a Charter right is saved by section 1: the law must have a pressing and substantial goal, be rationally connected to it, impair the right as little as possible, and its benefits must outweigh its harms.",
        """(?:the )?(?:victim(?:s'?)? impact statement)""" to "A written or spoken statement by the victim of a crime, read at sentencing, describing how the crime affected them.",
        """(?:a )?probation""" to "A sentence in which the offender stays in the community under conditions, such as reporting to a probation officer, for up to three years.",
        """(?:a )?(?:parole)""" to "Early, supervised release of a prisoner for the rest of their sentence, granted by the Parole Board of Canada. A life sentence for first degree murder has no parole for 25 years.",
    ).map { Triple(Regex("^(?:" + it.first + ")$"), it.second, it.first) }

    private fun out(body: String) = Answer.Text(heading = null, body = body, source = "Nimble law")

    fun answer(q: String): Answer? {
        for ((re, body) in facts) if (re.containsMatchIn(q)) return out(body)
        val t = q.replace(Regex(""" in (?:canadian |canada'?s? |bc |criminal |civil |contract |tort |family )?law$| in canada$"""), "")
        return Terms.answer(t, terms, emptyList())?.let { out(it) }
    }
}
